## Context and scope

This proposed decision supersedes [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).
The accepted record remains intact. Its subject-only rule can hide different problems
under one title and split the same problem across different titles.
The [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says “one issue, one fix queued”; its computed
view shows otherwise. Read computed results before relying on that prose.

This analysis covers the 2026-10-04 export of reports merged into #4471.
Deduplicate overlapping export pages by report ID, then filter for that ticket.
Use the tenant directory's locale and booking form, with the report's channel.
Browser locale and intake tags are guesses, not the cause oracle.
The table runs the supplied cause rules; counts belong in components.

## Findings and separate engineering tickets

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

**Parser:** the shared parser reads month first for day-first tenants.
For example R-41200 (harbour.dental, en-GB, app) describes 7 April becoming 4 July.
Fix the shared parser to interpret the tenant's date convention consistently.
Verify ambiguous and unambiguous dates through app and email, and booking-v2 forms.
A parser repair must not be treated as closing the separate form-hint problem.

**Form hint:** booking-v3 prints MM/DD/YYYY for day-first tenants, while its form
reads the entry in the tenant's order. This affects form reports from alder.physio,
copper.studio and pale.harbour. R-41230 is an example even though intake tagged it
as parser. Make the displayed hint agree with the form's actual tenant-aware parsing;
verify input, displayed hint and saved date together for each affected tenant.

<Callout kind="warn">
The footnoted reports describe a one-day move, an invite before booking, or another unusual
symptom. Their placement follows the supplied tenant-based classifier; it is not proof that
a month/day parser explains their bodies. Preserve their IDs and investigate timing,
time zones and source events before claiming those cases fixed. Do not invent a third
confirmed cause or silently remove them from the exported totals.
</Callout>

## Engineering outcome: 5 October

After the initial tickets were accepted, the course desk published an engineering
update: #4471 closed on 5 October. The **parser was fixed** by
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The **hint was closed as won't fix**
because the **booking-v3 form was retired**. These are different outcomes: retiring
a form does not mean its hint was repaired. Close the parser ticket as completed
and the hint ticket as not planned, preserving both historical report counts.

Source: the authenticated Challenge page, read 31 October; its update is transcribed
in `evidence/desk-update.txt`. This is the desk's reported disposition; we have not
independently tested a production parser deployment. The export remains historical
evidence, including the five unusual symptoms needing care when interpreting it.

## Queue history

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The received and merged series measure different events. The July merge spike includes
older reports; it is not evidence of a July onset or a fix. Reports continue through the
export cutoff. The export is a snapshot: refresh against the desk before closing work.

## Replacement triage rule

Read the body and identify tenant, locale, form version, channel and reproducible failure.
Merge only when evidence establishes the same cause. Keep unknown tenants or unclear
symptoms pending investigation rather than inferring a cause from the subject.
A shared subject can span causes; different subjects can share a cause.
The executable examples below demonstrate both boundaries, including the unknown-tenant case.

<DedupeExplorer />

## Consequences and review

Engineering gets a separate ticket and count for each confirmed cause. The desk must
read and investigate reports, preserve provenance and reconcile counts by ID.
Recheck the export and unresolved symptoms when new evidence arrives. Review the stale
triage page and replace its advice through this decision; a recent review date alone
cannot make unsupported prose true. No fix has been deployed or verified by this analysis.

